**PostToolUse fires after every Edit or Write tool call. A shell hook runs your formatter on the changed file and surfaces lint failures back to Claude without blocking the tool call.**

Claude Code's hook system has three events: `PreToolUse` (fires before, can block), `Stop` (fires at session end, can block with exit 2), and `PostToolUse` (fires after every matching tool call, can't block but its stdout returns to Claude's context). PostToolUse is the right place for per-edit feedback loops — format, lint, run the file's affected tests.

**Wire it in `.claude/settings.json`:**

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PostToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Edit|Write",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "bash .claude/hooks/post-edit.sh"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

**`.claude/hooks/post-edit.sh`:**

```bash
#!/bin/bash
# Claude Code writes tool context as JSON to stdin
FILE=$(cat | jq -r '.tool_input.file_path // empty')
[ -z "$FILE" ] || [ ! -f "$FILE" ] && exit 0

# Format in place (suppress output — just apply)
npx prettier --write "$FILE" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true

# Lint and report failures — stdout goes back to Claude's context
npx eslint "$FILE" --format compact --no-error-on-unmatched-pattern
```

Claude Code writes a JSON payload to the hook's stdin. The key fields for file tools are `tool_input.file_path` (the file being written) and `tool_name` (e.g., `Edit`). For Bash calls, the relevant field is `tool_input.command`. The payload also carries `tool_response` (the tool's result), `session_id`, and `cwd`.

**Key difference from PreToolUse:** PreToolUse fires before the edit — the file doesn't have its new content yet. PostToolUse fires after, so the file is already written and lintable.

**Key difference from the Stop hook:** Stop fires once at session end. PostToolUse fires after every Edit or Write in the session — it's for per-operation feedback, not a final gate.

**Limits:** PostToolUse runs synchronously before Claude sees the next turn. A formatter that takes 300 ms on each call is noticeable across a long editing session. Keep the script fast: lint only the changed file, not the whole project. If you need a heavier check (a full test suite or type-check pass), put it in the Stop hook instead, where it runs once.

**Sources:** [Claude Code hooks docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks) · [Hook stdin schema (pablolion.github.io)](https://pablolion.github.io/claude-hookwatch/reference/hook-stdin-schema.html) · [Claude Code Hooks as Automated Quality Gates (Understanding Data)](https://understandingdata.com/posts/claude-code-hooks-quality-gates/)

**Replace per-row tools with parameterized URI handlers: one `@mcp.resource` template exposes any variant of a data fetch, keeping your tool list focused on operations that actually need model reasoning.**

A tool list that includes `get_user`, `get_post`, `get_comment`, and `get_order` has four entries doing the same thing: fetch a row by ID. MCP resource templates replace that pattern. When a client wants a specific row, it calls `resources/read` on a parameterized URI instead of routing through a tool — no extra LLM turn required for the lookup.

## The pattern in FastMCP (Python)

```python
from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP

mcp = FastMCP("my-data-server")

@mcp.resource("users://{user_id}")
def get_user(user_id: str) -> str:
    return db.get_user(user_id)

@mcp.resource("posts://{post_id}")
def get_post(post_id: str) -> str:
    return db.get_post(post_id)
```

Clients discover available templates via `resources/templates/list`, then read a specific resource with `resources/read` on a concrete URI (`users://42`). The URI value maps directly to the function argument.

## Three things to know before wiring this in

1. **Resources are read-only by design.** Use them for fetches. Mutations (`create_user`, `update_post`, `delete_order`) stay as tools — that is the right separation.
2. **URI templates use RFC 6570 Level 1 syntax.** Most SDK implementations support simple `{variable}` placeholders only; complex expansions (`{+var}`, `{var*}`) are not guaranteed across all clients.
3. **Not every MCP client surfaces templates the same way.** Claude Code reads them, but some clients don't expose templates in their UI panel. Test with [MCP Inspector](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/inspector) before relying on template discovery in your workflow.

**When resources don't fit:** if the client needs to discover what IDs are available (not just read a known one), pair the template with a `list_*` tool that returns the available IDs. The template handles the fetch; the tool handles enumeration.

**Sources:** [FastMCP resource templates — gofastmcp.com](https://gofastmcp.com/docs/v3/servers/resources) · [MCP specification: Resources — modelcontextprotocol.io](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/resources) · [MCP Python SDK: URI templates — py.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io](https://py.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/servers/uri-templates/)

**For jobs that touch multiple independent files, run a review across N dimensions, or need a fan-out + verification pass, the Workflow tool runs a typed JavaScript script that orchestrates subagents deterministically — with phase progress, resume from failure, and cached replay.**

Agent teams let subagents message each other. Workflows are different: you write the orchestration in JavaScript, fan out as many `agent()` calls as you need, and the harness runs them in parallel. The script is deterministic — same inputs, same execution graph. That makes failures debuggable and expensive parts reusable via cache.

**Three primitives:**

```javascript
export const meta = {
  name: "review-pr",
  description: "Review a PR across three dimensions",
  phases: [{ title: "Review" }, { title: "Verify" }],
}

// agent(): one subagent — returns text, or JSON if you pass schema:
// parallel(): all items run concurrently, waits for all to complete
// pipeline(): streaming — item 0 hits stage 2 while item 1 is still in stage 1

const DIMENSIONS = ["security", "performance", "correctness"]

const reviews = await parallel(
  DIMENSIONS.map(dim => () =>
    agent(`Review the diff for ${dim} issues`, {
      label: `review:${dim}`,
      phase: "Review",
      schema: FINDINGS_SCHEMA,
    })
  )
)

const verified = await pipeline(
  reviews.flat(),
  r => agent(`Verify this finding: ${r.title}`, { phase: "Verify" })
)
return { confirmed: verified.filter(v => v.isReal) }
```

**To run it:** include "workflow" or "ultracode" in your request, or ask Claude to "use a workflow." Live phase progress appears in `/workflows`.

**To resume after a failure:** pass `resumeFromRunId: "wf_..."` on the next invocation. Completed `agent()` calls replay from cache; only the failed call and those after it re-run. This is the main reason to prefer `pipeline()` over `parallel()` for large jobs — intermediate results cache between stages.

**What it won't do:** Each `agent()` call starts with zero context, so prompts must be self-contained — a subagent in one call cannot read what another wrote unless you pass the content explicitly. Each agent also draws from your session's token budget, so a 20-agent workflow is a large spend. The harness guideline is to keep scripts under 10 agents for routine work; go larger only when the task explicitly needs it.

**Sources:** [Claude Code docs — code.claude.com](https://code.claude.com/docs) · [Dynamic workflows announcement — InfoQ](https://www.infoq.com/news/2026/06/dynamic-workflows-claude-code/) · [Claude Code Workflows: Deterministic orchestration — alexop.dev](https://alexop.dev/posts/claude-code-workflows-deterministic-orchestration/)

**Claude Code expands `${VAR}` and `${VAR:-default}` in every `.mcp.json` string value at server startup — credentials stay in env vars, the config file is safe to commit.**

If you've ever pasted a database URL or API token directly into `.mcp.json` to get a server running, the expansion syntax removes the reason to do that. Claude Code substitutes every `${VAR}` reference before the server process starts.

**The pattern:**

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "my-api": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "${MY_API_URL:-http://localhost:4000/mcp}",
      "headers": {
        "Authorization": "Bearer ${MY_API_TOKEN}"
      }
    },
    "postgres": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["@modelcontextprotocol/server-postgres"],
      "env": {
        "POSTGRES_URL": "${DATABASE_URL}"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Set `MY_API_TOKEN` and `DATABASE_URL` in your shell profile, in a `.env` file (kept in `.gitignore`), or via a secrets manager.

**With 1Password CLI**, the `op run` wrapper sets variables from the vault just before Claude Code starts:

```bash
op run --env-file=.mcp.env -- claude
```

Where `.mcp.env` contains references like `MY_API_TOKEN=op://Vault/api-credentials/token`. The raw token never touches the filesystem outside the vault.

**Where it breaks:**

- Expansion runs once at startup. If a token rotates mid-session, restart Claude Code to pick up the new value.
- A missing variable with no default expands to the literal `${VAR}` string — the server starts but every tool call fails at the connection step, not at launch. Use `${VAR:-}` (empty-string default) if you want an earlier failure, or validate the var in a shell script before invoking Claude Code.
- The existing `.claude/settings.local.json` allow-rule problem (storing full shell commands including inline credentials) is a separate issue — `${VAR}` in `.mcp.json` does not protect against that path.

**Sources:** [Claude Code MCP configuration docs — code.claude.com](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp-configuration) · [Securing MCP credentials with 1Password — Towards AI](https://pub.towardsai.net/secure-your-mcp-credentials-2443b4eded34) · [MCP server setup guide — builder.io](https://www.builder.io/blog/claude-code-mcp-servers)

**Claude picks tools by their `description` field, not their `name` — a one-liner like "Gets stock data" leaves the model guessing whether to call the tool at all and how to fill its parameters.**

**What you'll be able to do after this:** Write tool definitions that Claude invokes correctly on the first call, without system-prompt coaching.

- The `description` should answer four questions: what the tool does, when to invoke it, what it returns, and what the return shape looks like.
- Each parameter's `description` should state its format, valid values, and an example — not just restate the name.
- An optional `input_examples` array shows Claude how to fill a complex schema; examples must be schema-valid or the API returns a 400 (not supported on server-side MCP tools).

**Before and after**

A tool that misfires consistently:

```python
{
    "name": "get_stock_price",
    "description": "Gets stock data",
    "input_schema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
            "ticker": {"type": "string"},
            "period": {"type": "string"}
        },
        "required": ["ticker"]
    }
}
```

After the description treatment:

```python
{
    "name": "get_stock_price",
    "description": (
        "Returns the closing price for a publicly traded US equity over a time window. "
        "Call this when the user asks about a stock price or historical returns. "
        "Returns JSON: {'ticker': str, 'currency': str, "
        "'prices': [{'date': 'YYYY-MM-DD', 'close': float}]}, newest first."
    ),
    "input_schema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
            "ticker": {
                "type": "string",
                "description": "Exchange ticker symbol, uppercase — e.g. 'AAPL', 'MSFT'. US equities only."
            },
            "period": {
                "type": "string",
                "description": "Lookback window. One of '1d', '1w', '1m', '3m', '1y'. Defaults to '1m' if omitted.",
                "enum": ["1d", "1w", "1m", "3m", "1y"]
            }
        },
        "required": ["ticker"]
    }
}
```

**What changed:** The `description` now answers all four questions. Each parameter description adds format constraints and an example. The `enum` on `period` removes an entire category of invalid input errors.

**Where it still goes wrong:** A well-written description doesn't help if the system prompt contradicts it, or if two tools have overlapping names and descriptions — Claude distributes calls unpredictably across near-synonyms. Tool names must match `^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$`; names that don't will cause a 400, not a model error. `input_examples` are supported on client-side tools only, not on server-side MCP tools.

**Sources:** [Define tools — platform.claude.com](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/define-tools) · [Tool Schema Design — open-exam-prep.com](https://open-exam-prep.com/study-guides/anthropic-ccdv-f/ch10-tools-mcp-architecture/tool-schema-definition) · [Claude tool use: advanced patterns — DEV Community](https://dev.to/claudeguide/claude-tool-use-advanced-patterns-for-production-7di)

**AI design tools generate visually correct output reliably — semantically correct output inconsistently. One Playwright test file running axe-core WCAG 2.2 AA scans catches the gap before it ships.**

**What you'll be able to do after this:** Add a Playwright test that runs axe-core WCAG 2.2 AA scans against newly imported AI-generated components, block CI on critical violations, and understand what automated testing catches versus what still needs a human pass.

**Three-bullet takeaway:**
- `npm install @axe-core/playwright` wires automated accessibility scans into any existing Playwright setup in one file
- `.withTags(['wcag2a','wcag2aa','wcag21a','wcag21aa'])` targets WCAG 2.2 A/AA criteria only — avoiding vendor-default extra rules that your team has not agreed to enforce
- Automation reliably catches missing alt text, broken form labels, contrast failures, and misused ARIA — it does not catch logical focus order, motion sensitivity, or screen-reader narrative flow; those need a manual pass

**Setup:**

```bash
npm install @axe-core/playwright
```

**Test file:**

```ts
// tests/a11y.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';
import AxeBuilder from '@axe-core/playwright';

test('imported AI component passes WCAG 2.2 AA scan', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/components/preview');
  const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
    .withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa', 'wcag21a', 'wcag21aa'])
    .analyze();
  const critical = results.violations.filter(
    v => v.impact === 'critical' || v.impact === 'serious'
  );
  expect(critical).toHaveLength(0);
});
```

**Workflow:** Run this against each newly imported AI-generated component before merging. The most common failures from Claude Design and v0 output: images without `alt`, form inputs without `<label>`, interactive `<div>` elements missing `role` and `tabindex`, and color contrast violations in generated dark-mode variants.

**Gate incrementally:** Filter by `impact === 'critical'` first to unblock today; add `'serious'` once the backlog is cleared. Do not comment out the test to pass CI — that is a signal the generated component needs a regeneration pass, not a test-silencing pass. Claude Code can fix most axe violations in a single prompt once you paste in the violation report.

**The limit:** axe-core catches roughly 40–57% of WCAG criteria automatically — Deque's own measurement. It cannot see keyboard focus order, screen-reader reading order, or whether button labels communicate purpose in context. Pair the scan with a ten-minute keyboard navigation check on any component before it goes into a design system.

**Sources:** [Official Playwright accessibility testing docs — playwright.dev](https://playwright.dev/docs/accessibility-testing) · [axe-core WCAG coverage research — deque.com](https://www.deque.com/axe/core-documentation/) · [AI accessibility testing CI workflow — developertoolkit.ai](https://developertoolkit.ai/en/workflows/test/accessibility-testing/)

**Your CI catches the lint errors Claude Code creates because the agent writes files without running the linter — a PostToolUse hook runs `eslint --fix` after every edit and feeds the result back to Claude in the same turn.**

When Claude Code edits a file, it moves on. Lint errors surface at the next explicit `pnpm lint` call or at CI — not immediately. PostToolUse hooks run after each tool call completes and pipe their stdout back to Claude as a tool feedback message, so the model sees lint status before it declares the task done.

**How PostToolUse hooks work**

Hooks are shell commands registered in `.claude/settings.json` under `hooks.PostToolUse`. Each hook runs synchronously after the matched tool call.

- **`matcher`**: a regex matching the tool name, e.g., `"Edit|Write"` or `"Bash"`
- **`command`**: the shell command to run. It receives the tool input as JSON on stdin
- **Exit code 0**: allow the turn to continue
- **Exit code 2**: block the action; Claude sees the hook's reason
- **Stdout**: returned to Claude as a feedback message in the same turn

**Hook configuration**

Add to `.claude/settings.json`:
```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PostToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Edit|Write",
        "command": "node .claude/hooks/lint-after-edit.mjs"
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

`.claude/hooks/lint-after-edit.mjs`:
```js
import { execSync } from 'child_process';
import { readFileSync } from 'fs';

const raw = readFileSync('/dev/stdin', 'utf8');
const toolInput = JSON.parse(raw || '{}');
const file = toolInput.file_path ?? '';

// Only lint JS/TS files
if (!/\.(ts|tsx|js|mjs|cjs)$/.test(file)) process.exit(0);

try {
  execSync(`eslint --fix "${file}" 2>&1`, { stdio: 'pipe' });
  // No output = clean file; nothing fed back to Claude
} catch (e) {
  // Unfixable issues remain — report back so Claude can address them
  const msg = e.stdout?.toString() ?? e.stderr?.toString() ?? '';
  if (msg.trim()) console.log(`ESLint issues remain in ${file}:
${msg.trim()}`);
}
process.exit(0); // Always let the turn continue
```

**What Claude does with the feedback**

When the hook prints to stdout, Claude sees it as a feedback block in the turn. If you pair the hook with a task instruction like "fix lint errors as you go," Claude addresses the unfixable issues it sees and retries. Without that instruction it acknowledges the feedback but may not rewrite immediately.

**Limits**

- PostToolUse runs synchronously — a slow lint or test command adds real latency to every edit. Keep hooks under 2 seconds or scope them narrowly by extension.
- Stdout is advisory, not authoritative. Whether the model acts on it depends on task context. For hard requirements, use a PreToolUse check or an explicit lint step in your task workflow.
- Hooks in `.claude/settings.json` are committed and apply to everyone. Put per-developer preferences in `.claude/settings.local.json` (gitignored) to avoid forcing the hook on teammates with different workflows.

**Sources:** [Claude Code hooks reference](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks) · [CC Hooks: The Deterministic Layer Around Your Agent (Blake Crosley)](https://blakecrosley.com/blog/claude-code-hooks-explained) · [Complete Guide to Claude Code Hooks — MLOps Community](https://home.mlops.community/public/blogs/the-complete-guide-to-claude-code-hooks-automating-your-ai-coding-workflow)

**`npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector` lets you call any MCP tool interactively without a live Claude session; Vitest + in-memory transports run the same calls headlessly in your CI pipeline.**

Every MCP server you ship has the same gap: you test the tool handler logic in isolation, then test it against a live Claude session, but nothing tests the full protocol layer without Claude. Two tools close that gap.

**MCP Inspector — visual and CLI tester**

```bash
npx -y @modelcontextprotocol/inspector tsx src/index.ts
```

Opens a browser UI where you fire tool calls, inspect input/output schemas, and read raw JSON-RPC traffic. For CI, `--cli` mode runs a single call and exits:

```bash
npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector --cli tsx src/index.ts \
  --tool myTool --args '{"key":"value"}'
```

**In-memory transport for unit tests — no process spawning, no live Claude**

The MCP TypeScript SDK includes `InMemoryTransport`, which connects a test client directly to your server without stdio or HTTP:

```typescript
import { InMemoryTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/inMemory.js";
import { Client } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/index.js";
import { createServer } from "./server.js";

const [clientTransport, serverTransport] = InMemoryTransport.createLinkedPair();
const server = createServer();
await server.connect(serverTransport);
const client = new Client({ name: "test", version: "1.0" }, {});
await client.connect(clientTransport);
const result = await client.callTool({ name: "myTool", arguments: { key: "value" } });
expect(result.content[0].text).toBe("expected output");
```

`createLinkedPair()` wires client and server in memory — the full MCP protocol runs but no stdio or HTTP is involved. Add this to your Vitest suite and your MCP tools now have regression tests that run in milliseconds.

**What this won't catch:** Neither the Inspector nor in-memory tests simulate multi-turn agent context, tool chaining across sessions, or how Claude decides which tool to call. Those need real integration tests against a Claude session — the Inspector is for protocol-layer correctness, not agent behavior.

**Sources:** [MCP Inspector — modelcontextprotocol.io](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/tools/inspector) · [MCP TypeScript SDK](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk) · [MCP Server Testing and Quality Assurance — Daniel Vaughan](https://codex.danielvaughan.com/2026/05/26/mcp-server-testing-quality-assurance-unit-integration-inspector/)

**If your MCP tool only reads static data and returns it unchanged, it's a resource — converting it removes tool-call overhead and lets Claude browse the data without consuming a conversation turn.**

The MCP spec draws a sharp line between **tools** (actions Claude explicitly invokes, with inputs and side effects) and **resources** (addressable data Claude or the host can read, like a filesystem). Most MCP servers built from tutorials default to tools — even for things that just read a file or return a schema that never changes.

The practical cost: each tool call consumes a `tool_use` and `tool_result` block in the conversation, counts against context, and forces Claude to reason explicitly about "should I call this now?" For tools that retrieve the same static data every time, that overhead fires on every request.

Resources sidestep this. Claude (or the host client) can list available resources and read them on demand without a tool_use block. With FastMCP:

```python
# Before: a tool that reads the same file every time
@mcp.tool()
def get_database_schema() -> str:
    return open("schema.sql").read()

# After: a resource — same data, available without a tool_use block
@mcp.resource("db://schema")
def database_schema() -> str:
    return open("schema.sql").read()
```

The decision rule:

- **Tool** → takes inputs, performs an action, may change state (query a database, run a command, write a file)
- **Resource** → returns data you would read in a documentation page: schema, API reference, project rules, config

A quick check: delete the tool name from your docstring. If what remains is "returns the contents of X," it should be a resource.

**Limits:** Resources aren't re-fetched automatically when the underlying data changes — there is no cache invalidation. For data that updates frequently (live query results, test output), keep it as a tool. Converting to a resource also does not eliminate token cost: a large schema still consumes context tokens when Claude reads it; the saving is in conversation-turn overhead, not token count. And not all MCP host clients expose resource browsing in the same way — verify your target client (Claude Code, your SDK-based host) supports resources before converting a production tool.

**Sources:** [MCP resources concept](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/concepts/resources) · [FastMCP resources](https://github.com/jlowin/fastmcp) · [MCP spec — tools vs resources](https://spec.modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/server/resources/)
